INTRODUCTION

	fetchconfig is a Perl script for retrieving the configuration
	of multiple devices. It has been tested under Linux, AIX and
	Windows, and currently supports a variety of devices, including
	Cisco IOS, Cisco Catalyst, Cisco ASA, Cisco PIX, FortiGate
	Firewalls, ProCurve, Alcatel Ethernet Routers (aka
	Riverstone), Dell PowerConnect Switches, Terayon 3200/3500
	CMTS, Datacom DmSwitch Switches, HP MSR Routers, Mikrotik
	Routers, Tellabs MSR Routers, Juniper EX Switch JunOS, Oracle
	Acme Packet, Audiocodes Mediant, Cisco IOS XR, NEC Univerge
	IX, Hirschmann Railswitch, Zyxel switches, PLANET managed
	switches, Aruba CX switches, Cisco Nexus switches, AudioCodes
	Mediant SBCs, TP-Link Easy Smart switches, HP ProCurve 1700 series
	switches, and HP ProCurve
	ProVision (incl. 4000M/8000M) over SNMP+TFTP.

IMPORTANT CHANGE IN 9.55 (filename_append_suffix)

	Starting with 9.55, the filename_append_suffix option must
	start with a "." (e.g. ".txt"). A device whose suffix lacks the
	leading dot, or contains "/" or whitespace, now FAILS its backup
	with an error and writes nothing. If your device table carries a
	dotless suffix from an older version, fix it before the first
	9.55 run. Also: do not add, remove or change a suffix on a device
	that already has backups; use "fetchconfig.pl -S" to find devices
	whose existing backups already mix suffixes. See
	filename_append_suffix under OPTIONS.

IMPORTANT CHANGE IN 9.53 (affects every device)

	Starting with 9.53, saved configuration files end with a
	trailing newline on the last line, matching the device's own
	output (previously the last line had no terminating newline).
	This makes the files proper POSIX text files and matches what
	you get when downloading a config directly from a device's web
	UI.

	This change touches EVERY model, not just one. Because the file
	content changes by that one final byte, the FIRST backup run
	after upgrading to 9.53 will report every device as
	"changed" (and, with changes_only, will save a new copy). This
	is a one-time effect of the newline fix, not real configuration
	drift; the second run onward compares clean again.

