INSTALLATION (UNIX/Linux)

	1. fetchconfig.pl expects a Perl interpreter at /usr/bin/perl.

	2. Perl module requirements. See PERL MODULE REQUIREMENTS
	below for the full list and which ones each kind of device
	needs. In short: every run needs Net::Telnet and Net::SMTP;
	SSH devices additionally need Net::OpenSSH; the web-UI models
	need LWP::UserAgent and HTTP::Request::Common; and the
	procurve-snmp model needs Net::SNMP and Net::TFTP. All the
	other modules fetchconfig uses are part of the Perl core
	(5.26 and later; tested on 5.26, 5.28 and 5.40). Install
	whichever non-core ones you need
	(the file DEPENDENCIES lists every module, core vs CPAN for Perl
	5.26/5.28/5.40, broken down by feature),
	e.g.:

		cpan install Net::Telnet Net::OpenSSH

	3. Unpack the fetchconfig tarball at a suitable
        location. Example:

	[ fetchconfig tarball available at /tmp/fetchconfig-0.0.tar.gz ]

	cd /usr/local
	tar xzf /tmp/fetchconfig-0.0.tar.gz
	ln -s fetchconfig-0.0 fetchconfig

	[ fetchconfig is now available under /usr/local/fetchconfig ]

INSTALLATION (Windows)

	1. fetchconfig.pl expects to find a Perl interpreter in the PATH.

	2. Perl module requirements. See PERL MODULE REQUIREMENTS
	below for the full list and which ones each kind of device
	needs. In short: every run needs Net::Telnet and Net::SMTP;
	SSH devices additionally need Net::OpenSSH; the web-UI models
	need LWP::UserAgent and HTTP::Request::Common; and the
	procurve-snmp model needs Net::SNMP and Net::TFTP. All the
	other modules fetchconfig uses are part of the Perl core
	(5.26 and later; tested on 5.26, 5.28 and 5.40). Install
	whichever non-core ones you need,
	e.g.:

		cpan install Net::Telnet Net::OpenSSH

	3. Unpack the fetchconfig tarball at a suitable
	location.  From the command line, gzip and tar (links
	below) can be used.
	http://www.gzip.org
	http://gnuwin32.sourceforge.net/packages/tar.htm

	Alternatively, some commercial programs (such as WinZip)
	offer a GUI.

	Command Line Example:

	[ fetchconfig tarball available at c:\temp\fetchconfig-0.0.tar.gz ]

	c:
	cd \
	gzip -cd c:\temp\fetchconfig-0.0.tar.gz | tar xvf -
	move fetchconfig-0.0 fetchconfig

	[ fetchconfig is now available under c:\fetchconfig ]

PERL MODULE REQUIREMENTS

	fetchconfig runs on Perl 5.28 or later. Most of the modules it
	uses are part of the Perl core on those versions and need no
	separate installation: Text::ParseWords, POSIX, IO::Handle,
	Time::HiRes, MIME::Base64, FindBin, File::Temp, File::Path,
	File::Compare and Net::SMTP.

	The following modules are NOT in the Perl core and must be
	installed separately, but only if you use the devices that need
	them:

	Net::Telnet	Needed on every run. Used for telnet devices,
			and also to drive the pseudo-terminal for all
			SSH devices. In practice fetchconfig cannot back
			up any device without it.

	Net::SMTP	Needed on every run. It is part of the "libnet"
			distribution and ships with most Perl
			installations, but it is not in the Perl core.
			Note that fetchconfig loads its mail/reporting
			code on every run - it writes the per-device
			status file (see STATUS FILE) even when no
			e-mail is configured - so Net::SMTP must be
			present regardless of whether summary e-mail is
			enabled. No SMTP connection is made unless an
			"email:" line is configured.

	Net::OpenSSH	Needed for all SSH-based models (cisco-ios-ssh,
			cisco-asa-ssh, comware-ssh, procurve-ssh,
			planet-ssh, aruba-cx-ssh and any other *-ssh
			model). Not needed if you only use telnet, web
			UI or SNMP devices.

	LWP::UserAgent, HTTP::Request::Common
			Needed for the web-UI models (tplink-web-sg105e,
			procurve-web-1700), which have no CLI. Both come
			with the "libwww-perl" distribution.

	Net::SNMP, Net::TFTP
			Needed for the procurve-snmp model (HP ProCurve
			ProVision, including the 4000M/8000M chassis),
			which backs up over SNMP + TFTP.

	IO::Socket::SSL (and its prerequisite Net::SSLeay)
			Needed only if you enable TLS for the summary
			e-mail ("email: tls=starttls" or "tls=ssl").
			Plain SMTP does not need it. Mozilla::CA is
			optional, for a CA bundle where the system has
			none.

	To install the non-core modules you need, for example:

		cpan install Net::Telnet Net::OpenSSH
		cpan install LWP::UserAgent Net::SNMP Net::TFTP

USAGE

	1. Edit the file "device_table.example" according to your needs,
        and save it as "device_table". Example:

	[ fetchconfig available under /usr/local/fetchconfig ]

	cd /usr/local/fetchconfig
	cp device_table.example device_table
	vi device_table

	2. Run fetchconfig.pl, as in the following example:

	[ fetchconfig available under /usr/local/fetchconfig ]

	cd /usr/local/fetchconfig
	./fetchconfig.pl -devices=device_table

	The fetchconfig.pl script will scan the "device_table" file,
	trying to retrieve the configuration for every device, storing
	configuration files under the "repository".

	By default the devices are fetched one after another, in
	device-table order. With many devices the run time is dominated
	by the network sessions; "-P N" fetches N devices concurrently -
	see PARALLEL FETCHING below.

DEVICE TABLE

	The device table assigned with the '-devices=' switch is a text
        file that supports two types of lines:

	1. Default-options lines assign default options to a device
        model. The following example adds some default options to the
        "cisco-ios" model:

	#default	model		model-default-options
	#
	default:        cisco-ios       user=backup,pass=fran,enable=jose

	2. Device lines define the attributes of every device and
        optionally assign device-specific options. The following
        example defines a device named "vpn-gw":

	#model		dev-unique-id	device-host	[device-options]
	#
	cisco-ios       vpn-gw          192.168.0.1     keep=10,changes_only=1

	The dev-unique-id must be:
	a) an unique identifier across all devices;
	b) a valid filesystem's directory name.

EMAIL NOTIFICATIONS

	fetchconfig.pl can e-mail a summary of a run's backup results
	as an HTML table (Device-ID, Date, Time, State, Size,
	Changes). This is entirely optional: unless an "email:" line
	is present, no SMTP connection is ever attempted.

	1. Add an "email:" line to the DEFAULT OPTIONS SECTION (it is
	independent of any device model):

	#email:	options
	#
	email:	from=config.backup@acme.com,to=admin@acme.com,smtp=mail.acme.com

	from		Mandatory. The envelope/header sender address.

	to		Mandatory. The recipient who receives the
			status of every device backed up in the run.
			Multiple addresses can be given separated by
			';' (a comma cannot be used, since commas
			already separate options).

	smtp		Mandatory. The SMTP server, as "host" or
			"host:port" (default port 25). Plain SMTP
			only -- no encryption.

	user		Optional. SMTP AUTH LOGIN username.

	password	Optional. SMTP AUTH LOGIN password. Only
			used (and AUTH attempted) when "user" is
			also given.

	footer		Optional. The copyright/attribution text shown in
			the summary e-mail's footer. The " - Version <n>"
			that follows it is fixed and always appended. If
			the value contains a comma, wrap it in double
			quotes (the quotes are stripped); an unquoted,
			comma-free value needs none. Default:
			"(c) 2006-2026 Everton da Silva Marques, Rainer
			Tammer and others".

	port		Optional. TCP port of the SMTP server. Works
			with every tls setting. Precedence: an explicit
			"port=" wins over a port given as part of the
			host ("smtp=mail.acme.com:587"), which wins over
			the default. The default follows the TLS mode:
			25 for tls=off and tls=starttls, 465 for tls=ssl -
			so "tls=ssl" works without also setting the port.
			Must be 1-65535.
	tls		Optional. Transport security for the SMTP
			connection: "off" (default - plain SMTP, the
			historical behaviour), "starttls" (connect in
			clear, then upgrade with STARTTLS; typically
			port 25 or 587) or "ssl" (TLS from the start;
			typically port 465). Both TLS modes need the
			IO::Socket::SSL module (which needs Net::SSLeay);
			plain SMTP needs neither. If TLS is requested but
			the module is missing, or the TLS handshake fails,
			the mail is NOT sent - fetchconfig never falls
			back to plaintext, as that would silently defeat
			the point of enabling TLS. Note that AUTH LOGIN
			sends the password base64-encoded (not encrypted);
			use "tls" whenever the SMTP server is not on a
			trusted network.

	The "email:" line may be repeated; later values overwrite
	earlier ones for the same key (same rule as "default:").

	2. Optionally, override the recipient for one device with the
	device-specific "to=" option (available for all models):

	#model		dev-unique-id	hostname	device-options
	#
	parks		lab209r		10.30.0.6	to=lab-owner@acme.com

	A device whose recipient was overridden this way is left out
	of no one's mail: it is bundled with every other device
	sharing that same override recipient into one separate
	e-mail, sent only to that recipient, containing just their
	own device(s). The globally configured recipient (email:
	to=...) still receives the status of every device processed
	in the run, including overridden ones.

	3. The "Changes" column only reflects a changed-vs-previous
	comparison for devices where the run already performs one
	(changes_only=1, or on_fetch_run/on_fetch_cat set); for other
	devices it is reported as "n/a" -- no extra repository scan
	is performed just for this report.

EMBEDDING DEVICE TABLE INTO COMMAND LINE

	Valid lines for the device table file can also be passed
	directly through the -line=string command line switch.

	Example:
	/path/to/fetchconfig.pl -line="default: tellabs-msr user=backup,pass=s&cr&t,timeout=10,keep=5,changes_only=0,fetch_timeout=1200,filename_append_suffix=.txt,repository=/path/to/repo" -line="tellabs-msr lab-router 10.0.0.1"

RETRIEVING A PREVIOUSLY BACKED UP CONFIG

	In addition to fetching configuration from devices,
	fetchconfig.pl can also retrieve a configuration file
	already saved in the repository, without contacting the
	device again. This is done with the "-g" switch.

	This is useful for scripting: e.g. dumping the latest
	config of a device to stdout, comparing today's config
	against yesterday's, or pulling out an older backup by
	its position in the history.

	1. Retrieve the latest (most recent) backup for a device,
        printing it to stdout:

	[ fetchconfig available under /usr/local/fetchconfig ]

	cd /usr/local/fetchconfig
	./fetchconfig.pl -devices=device_table -g fw01.acme.net

	2. Retrieve an older backup, by passing "-n" together with
        "-g":

	./fetchconfig.pl -devices=device_table -g fw01.acme.net -n 2

	3. List every backup on file for a device, to find out which
        "-n"/"-m" number to use:

	./fetchconfig.pl -devices=device_table -l fw01.acme.net

	Sample output (tab-separated: number, size in bytes, path):

	1	28221	/usr/local/fetchconfig/config/202608/20260824/fw01.acme.net/fw01.acme.net.run.20260824.001105CEST
	2	28221	/usr/local/fetchconfig/config/202608/20260823/fw01.acme.net/fw01.acme.net.run.20260823.001105CEST

	The numbers in the first column are exactly the numbers "-n"
	and "-m" expect (1=latest, 2=next most recent, ...).

	4. Check whether any of a device's backups are 0 bytes long
        (e.g. a fetch that connected but failed to save anything):

	./fetchconfig.pl -devices=device_table -z fw01.acme.net

	If any are found, they are listed in the same format as
        "-l" above, and fetchconfig.pl exits with status 1; if
        none are found, nothing is printed and the exit status is 0.

	5. Run the same 0-byte check across every device in one go,
        instead of one device at a time, by using "-Z" (no dev_id)
        instead of "-z dev_id":

	./fetchconfig.pl -devices=device_table -Z

	Sample output if e.g. one device has an empty backup
        (tab-separated: dev_id, number, size in bytes, path):

	fw01.acme.net	1	0	/usr/local/fetchconfig/config/202608/20260825/fw01.acme.net/fw01.acme.net.run.20260825.001105CEST

	As with "-z", nothing is printed and the exit status is 0
        if no device has a 0-byte backup. A device whose repository
        can't be read is logged (to stderr) and skipped, without
        aborting the check for the remaining devices; that alone
        does not make "-Z" exit non-zero -- only an actual 0-byte
        backup does.

	6. Find backed up configs on disk that no longer have a
        matching device in the device table (e.g. a device that was
        decommissioned or renamed, leaving its old backups behind),
        with "-o":

	./fetchconfig.pl -devices=device_table -o

	Sample output if e.g. one old device's backups are found
        (tab-separated: dev_id, file count, total size in bytes,
        path to one date's worth of that device's backups):

	old-switch01	1	28221	/usr/local/fetchconfig/config/202607/20260715/old-switch01

	As with "-z"/"-Z", nothing is printed and the exit status is
        0 if nothing orphaned is found. "-o" scans every
        "repository=" path configured by a device in the currently
        loaded device table(s) (deduplicated -- most setups share
        one or a few repository paths); a repository path used only
        by a device no longer in the table won't be found this way,
        since there's nothing left to read that path from.

	To actually delete what "-o" found, add "-D". Since this is
        destructive, preview it first by also adding "-T" -- with
        "-T", the delete commands are only logged (to stderr), not
        run:

	./fetchconfig.pl -devices=device_table -o -D -T

	Once the preview looks right, drop "-T" to delete for real:

	./fetchconfig.pl -devices=device_table -o -D

	The tab-separated listing on stdout (or "-f file") is the
        same either way, so a script parsing it doesn't need to
        care whether "-D"/"-T" were used; what "-D" does (or, with
        "-T", would do) to each orphaned directory is logged
        separately. "-D" requires "-o"; "-T" requires "-D" (both
        report an error and exit otherwise). With "-D" but not
        "-T", the exit status changes meaning: 1 if any deletion
        failed, 0 if nothing needed deleting or everything found
        was deleted successfully (with "-T", or with plain "-o",
        the exit status still just reflects whether anything
        orphaned was found).

	6b. Check whether a device's backups have inconsistent
        filename suffixes (e.g. after filename_append_suffix was
        introduced on a device that already had backups), by using
        "-s dev_id", or "-S" to check every loaded device at once:

	./fetchconfig.pl -devices=device_table -s fw01.acme.net
	./fetchconfig.pl -devices=device_table -S

	Nothing is printed and the exit status is 0 if every device's
        backups share one suffix (or none). Otherwise each inconsistent
        device is listed, one line per distinct suffix, and the exit
        status is 1.

	6c. Find (and, with "-D", remove) empty directories left behind
        in the repositories, e.g. after deleting old backups by hand:

	./fetchconfig.pl -devices=device_table -e
	./fetchconfig.pl -devices=device_table -e -D -T   (preview)
	./fetchconfig.pl -devices=device_table -e -D      (delete)

	7. Save the retrieved config to a file instead of printing
        it to stdout, by adding "-f":

	./fetchconfig.pl -devices=device_table -g fw01.acme.net -n 2 -f config.file

	8. Compare two backed up versions of the same device,
        by adding "-m" together with "-n". The following example
        compares the latest backup ("-n 1") against the backup
        before it ("-m 2"):

	./fetchconfig.pl -devices=device_table -g fw01.acme.net -n 1 -m 2

	The output looks exactly like the output of the standard
	"diff" command run as "diff older newer", i.e. lines
	starting with "<" come from the "-m" (older) backup, lines
	starting with ">" come from the "-n" (newer) backup. As
	with "diff", the config file lists are otherwise unmodified.

	The device-unique-id passed to "-g" (or "-l") is looked up in the
	device table(s) given with "-devices=" and/or "-line="
	(see "EMBEDDING DEVICE TABLE INTO COMMAND LINE" above),
	exactly as when fetching; at least one of "-devices=" or
	"-line=" is therefore still required. Only the device's
	options (model, repository, etc.) are resolved from the
	device table -- the device itself is NOT contacted.

	Retrieval command-line flags:

	-g dev_id	Switches fetchconfig.pl to retrieval mode
                        for dev_id, instead of fetching configuration
                        from every device in the device table(s).
                        dev_id must match the dev-unique-id column
                        of a device line (see "DEVICE TABLE" above).
                        Mutually exclusive with "-l", "-z", "-Z" and "-o".

	-l dev_id	Switches fetchconfig.pl to listing mode for
                        dev_id: instead of retrieving a config,
                        prints one line per backup on file, newest
                        first, tab-separated as:
                        <number>\t<size in bytes>\t<path>
                        <number> is exactly what "-n"/"-m" expect.
                        Mutually exclusive with "-g", "-z", "-Z" and "-o".

	-z dev_id	Switches fetchconfig.pl to zero-byte check
                        mode for dev_id: scans every backup on file,
                        newest first, and prints only the ones that
                        are 0 bytes long, in the same tab-separated
                        format as "-l". If none of dev_id's backups
                        are 0 bytes, nothing is printed. Useful to
                        catch a fetch that connected successfully
                        but ended up saving an empty file.
                        fetchconfig.pl exits with status 1 if at
                        least one 0-byte backup was found, or 0 if
                        all backups are non-empty -- handy for
                        scripting/monitoring, e.g.:
                        fetchconfig.pl -devices=device_table -z dev_id \
                          || echo "ALERT: empty backup(s) for dev_id"
                        Mutually exclusive with "-g", "-l", "-Z" and "-o".

	-Z		Same as "-z", but takes no dev_id and checks
                        every device known from the currently loaded
                        "-devices="/"-line=" sources in one go,
                        instead of a single device. Each output line
                        is prefixed with the device id:
                        <dev_id>\t<number>\t<size in bytes>\t<path>
                        A device whose repository can't be resolved
                        or scanned is logged (to stderr) and skipped,
                        without aborting the check for the remaining
                        devices -- fetchconfig.pl's exit status still
                        follows the same convention as "-z" (1 if at
                        least one 0-byte backup was found anywhere,
                        0 otherwise); a device that could merely not
                        be scanned does not by itself cause a
                        non-zero exit. Mutually exclusive with "-g",
                        "-l", "-z" and "-o".

	-o		Switches fetchconfig.pl to orphaned-backup
                        check mode: lists backed up configs on disk
                        that have no matching dev-unique-id in the
                        currently loaded "-devices="/"-line="
                        sources. Every "repository=" path configured
                        by a currently loaded device is scanned
                        (deduplicated), and any device directory
                        found there whose name doesn't match a
                        currently known dev_id is reported - one
                        line per orphaned device directory (a device
                        can have more than one, one per date it was
                        backed up on), tab-separated as:
                        <dev_id-like name>\t<file count>\t<total bytes>\t<path>
                        Nothing is printed, and the exit status is 0,
                        if no orphaned backups are found.
                        fetchconfig.pl exits with status 1 if at
                        least one orphaned device directory was
                        found -- handy for scripting/monitoring, e.g.
                        finding stale backups worth cleaning up after
                        a device is decommissioned or renamed.
                        A repository path used only by a device no
                        longer in the device table can't be
                        discovered this way, since there's no
                        currently loaded device left to read that
                        path from. Mutually exclusive with "-g",
                        "-l", "-z" and "-Z".

	-D		Requires "-o" or "-e". With "-o": also DELETES every orphaned
                        device directory "-o" finds, not just lists
                        it. The tab-separated listing (stdout/"-f")
                        is unchanged; what's deleted (or, with "-T",
                        would be deleted) is logged separately (to
                        stderr): each backup file is deleted
                        individually by its exact name (never a
                        wildcard, never "rm -rf"/"rmdir /s"), logged
                        as "rm <file>", followed by "rmdir <path>"
                        for the now-empty directory. No shell command
                        is actually spawned - deletion uses Perl's
                        own unlink()/rmdir() directly; the logged
                        lines are for your benefit, not literally
                        executed. rmdir() only succeeds on a directory
                        that's genuinely empty, so anything left
                        behind unexpectedly (a file this scan didn't
                        account for) makes that directory's removal
                        fail loudly instead of being swept away
                        regardless. A repository root itself (as
                        opposed to an orphaned device directory under
                        it) is never deleted, even if something
                        unexpected made it look like a match.
                        fetchconfig.pl exits with status 1 if any
                        deletion failed, 0 if nothing needed deleting
                        or everything found was deleted successfully.

	-T		Requires "-D". Preview mode for both "-o -D" and
                        "-e -D": logs the delete commands "-D" would
                        run (see above) without actually deleting
                        anything. Exit status with "-T" is the same as
                        the plain check - "-o": 1 if anything orphaned
                        was found; "-e": 1 if any empty directory was
                        found; 0 otherwise - since "-T" doesn't attempt
                        any deletion to succeed or fail. Use it before a
                        bare "-o -D" or "-e -D" to see what would be
                        removed first.

	-e		Switches fetchconfig.pl to empty-directory check
                        mode: scans every "repository=" path configured
                        by a loaded device for empty directories in the
                        three-level layout
                        <repository>/YYYYMM/YYYYMMDD/<dev_id>/ and lists
                        them, one tab-separated row per directory:
                        <level>\t<path>   (level = device, day or month)
                        A directory is empty when it holds no entries at
                        all; the scan works bottom-up, so a day directory
                        whose only content is an empty device directory,
                        and a month directory whose only content is such
                        a day, are listed too. Only names of the expected
                        shape are candidates (YYYYMM, YYYYMMDD whose
                        first six digits match the month, and a dev_id-
                        shaped name starting with a letter or digit);
                        anything else in the repository, and the
                        repository root itself, is never touched. Such
                        leftovers typically come from deleting old
                        backups by hand (before 9.56 also from "-o -D",
                        which now cleans up after itself). Exit status is
                        1 if any empty directory was found, 0 otherwise.
                        Honours "-f". Mutually exclusive with "-g", "-l",
                        "-z", "-Z", "-o", "-s" and "-S".

                        With "-D", the empty directories are DELETED,
                        bottom-up: device directories first, then the
                        day directories that are empty by then, then the
                        month directories that are empty by then. Each
                        removal is ONE plain rmdir on the exact,
                        fully-qualified path - no wildcards, no recursive
                        delete; rmdir cannot remove a non-empty directory,
                        so files can never be taken along. Every command
                        is reported as "deleting: rmdir <path>"; the rows
                        gain a third column, deleted or failed. Exit
                        status is 1 if any rmdir failed, 0 otherwise.
                        "-e -D -T" reports "would delete: rmdir <path>"
                        for each directory and deletes nothing.

	-s dev_id	Switches fetchconfig.pl to suffix-consistency
                        check mode for dev_id: checks that all of the
                        device's backup files carry the same filename
                        suffix. Reports the device if its backups mix
                        suffixes - including "some with a suffix and some
                        without", which is what happens when
                        filename_append_suffix was added or changed after
                        backups already existed (see the OPTIONS
                        section). Prints one tab-separated line per
                        distinct suffix:
                        <dev_id>\t<suffix or (none)>\t<count>\t<example file>
                        Nothing is printed if the device is consistent.

                        A second step then compares the (single) suffix
                        the backups use against the filename_append_suffix
                        configured for the device in the device table,
                        and logs a warning if they differ - for instance
                        backups written before a suffix was configured,
                        or a suffix that was later changed. This step is
                        skipped for a device whose backups are already
                        inconsistent. The summary line and exit status
                        report the outcome:

                        0  backup suffixes are consistent, configured
                           suffix matches backup file suffixes
                        1  found N device(s) with inconsistent backup
                           suffixes, not checked against the configured
                           suffix
                        2  backup suffixes are consistent, configured
                           suffix does not match the backup file suffixes

                        so it can be scripted. Honours "-f". Mutually
                        exclusive with "-g", "-l", "-z", "-Z", "-o" and
                        "-S".

	-S		Like "-s", but takes no dev_id and checks every
                        device loaded via "-devices="/"-line=". "-S" is
                        a pure consistency check: it does NOT compare
                        against the configured filename_append_suffix
                        (that second step is "-s dev_id" only). Exit
                        status is 1 if any device's backups mix
                        suffixes, 0 otherwise. Mutually exclusive with
                        "-g", "-l", "-z", "-Z", "-o" and "-s".

                        Note that "-s" takes a dev_id; "-s -f file" is
                        rejected with "requires a device id" (the "-f"
                        would be taken for the device). Write
                        "-s dev_id -f file" or "-S -f file".

                        How "-s"/"-S" tell the timezone token from the
                        suffix: a backup is named
                        <dev_id>.run.YYYYMMDD.HHMMSS<tz><suffix>, and <tz>
                        is whatever strftime produced on the backup host -
                        it differs by platform and CHANGES WITH DAYLIGHT
                        SAVING TIME (AIX: CET in winter, CEST in summer;
                        older AIX: NFT/DFT). The check therefore parses
                        the tz token out first (numeric "+0200"/"-0500" on
                        Linux and modern Windows; a bare name "CEST" on
                        AIX; "-CEST" on Solaris; a multi-word name such as
                        "W. Europe Standard Time" on Windows) and only
                        then looks at what follows. The check ONLY WORKS
                        if the suffix is separated from the tz by a ".",
                        "-" or "_": "." is the rule since 9.55; "-" and
                        "_" are accepted as separators so that backups
                        written before that rule are still recognized. A
                        DST change is therefore NOT reported as a suffix
                        change. One inherent ambiguity: "-" followed by
                        uppercase letters is the Solaris tz form, so an
                        old suffix like "-BAK" on a timezone=hide backup
                        (no tz token) is read as a tz; "_BAK", "-bak", or
                        any suffix following a real tz token are
                        unambiguous.

	-n N		Optional. Selects which backup to retrieve,
                        counting from the most recent:
                        N=1 the latest (most recent) backup [default]
                        N=2 the backup before that
                        N=3 the backup before that, and so on.
                        If omitted, N=1 is assumed.

	-m M		Optional. Enables compare mode: instead of
                        printing backup N, fetchconfig.pl prints a
                        "diff" between backup M and backup N (M being
                        the older one, N being the newer one). M is
                        counted the same way as N (1=latest, 2=next
                        most recent, ...), and requires "-g".
                        M must be numerically higher than N (i.e.
                        M must refer to an older backup than N);
                        otherwise fetchconfig.pl reports an error
                        and exits without comparing anything.

	-f file		Optional. Writes the RESULT DATA to "file" instead
                        of printing it to stdout: the retrieved config
                        (with "-g"), the diff (with "-m"), or the
                        listing rows (with "-l", "-z", "-Z", "-o", "-s",
                        "-S"). The file is created/overwritten as
                        needed.

                        The file receives ONLY that data - never the
                        screen output. All "info:"/"debug:"/"error:"
                        lines, including the "checked N backup(s), ..."
                        summary and the "wrote ... to file" confirmation,
                        are log messages and always go to stderr, whether
                        or not "-f" is given.

                        For the listing/check modes ("-l", "-z", "-Z",
                        "-o", "-s", "-S") the result data ENDS WITH ONE
                        SUMMARY LINE prefixed by "#", carrying the same
                        text the run logs, e.g.
                          # dev=sw01: checked 2 backup(s), no zero-byte backups found
                        so the output is never empty and is
                        self-describing: a reader can tell "checked,
                        nothing found" from "nothing was written" without
                        the exit status or stderr. The rows before it
                        contain only the items FOUND (zero-byte backups,
                        orphaned directories, inconsistent suffixes; for
                        "-l", every backup). This trailer is part of the
                        result stream, so stdout and "-f" carry identical
                        content. It is NOT added to "-g" (a retrieved
                        config) or "-m" (a diff), which stay byte-exact.

                        IMPORTANT for anything that parses these
                        listings (e.g. fetchconfig-web): skip lines that
                        start with "#". Before 9.55 a check that found
                        nothing produced a 0-byte file.

	-?, -h, --help	Prints the usage/help text to stdout and exits
                        with status 0. (Before 9.55, "-?" was treated as
                        an unknown argument: it printed an error, the
                        usage to stderr, and ended with "Died at ...".)

	If dev_id is unknown, or if the device has no "repository"
	option configured, fetchconfig.pl reports an error and
	exits with a non-zero status; nothing is written to stdout
	or to "-f". The same applies if "-n" (or, with "-m", "-m"
	itself) refers to a backup that does not exist (e.g. "-n 5"
	when only 2 backups are on file) -- use "-l" beforehand if
	unsure how many backups exist.

	When "-m" is used, fetchconfig.pl's exit status follows the
	same convention as the "diff" command itself:
                        0	the two backups are identical
                        1	the two backups differ
                        (>1)	an error occurred (see above)
                        This makes "-m" convenient for scripting,
                        e.g. to detect whether a config actually
                        changed between two backups.

	Log/diagnostic messages (as produced by the existing
	fetchconfig.pl logger) are always sent to stderr, so
	stdout only ever carries the retrieved config content;
	this makes "-g" safe to use in pipelines and redirections,
	e.g.:

	./fetchconfig.pl -devices=device_table -g fw01.acme.net > latest.cfg
	./fetchconfig.pl -devices=device_table -g fw01.acme.net -n 1 > today.cfg
	./fetchconfig.pl -devices=device_table -g fw01.acme.net -n 2 > yesterday.cfg
	diff yesterday.cfg today.cfg

	...which "-m" does directly, without needing two separate
	retrievals and a manual diff:

	./fetchconfig.pl -devices=device_table -g fw01.acme.net -n 1 -m 2

	NOTE: compare mode ("-m") runs the standard "diff" utility
	behind the scenes, so a "diff" program must be available in
	PATH (this is the case on any regular Unix/Linux/AIX
	system).

DEVICE SUPPORT

	The following devices are known to be supported.  This
	is not a comprehensive list.  Other devices and versions
	are likely to work but have not been verified.

	Model Name	Devices/OS Supported
	----------	------------------------------------------------

	cisco-cat	CatOS switches

	cisco-ios	IOS routers
			2500 series, 2600 series, 2800 series,
			CS-516 (with show_cmd=wrterm option)

	cisco-asa	Cisco ASA firewalls

	cisco-pix	Cisco PIX firewalls

	cisco-iosxr	Cisco IOS XR

	fortigate	FortiOS 3.0

	parks		Parks 209R

	riverstone	Alcatel Ethernet Router 3100 (Riverstone RS3100)

	terayon-os	Terayon 3200/3500 CMTS

	dmswitch	Datacom DmSwitch

	3com-msr	3Com MSR routers

	mikrotik	MikroTik routers

	coriant-8600	Coriant 8600 Smart Router Series

	tellabs-msr	Tellabs MSR routers

	junos		Juniper EX Switch JunOS

	acme		Oracle Acme Packet

	mediant		Audiocodes Mediant

	nec-univerge-ix	NEC Univerge IX

	hirschmann	Hirschmann Railswitch (L2E software, telnet)

	zyxel		Zyxel switches (Cisco-IOS-like CLI, telnet)
	planet-ssh	PLANET managed switches (Cisco-IOS-like CLI, SSH;
			observed on IGS-4215-16P2T2S and IGS-5225-8P4S,
			also reported working on the M-Series -
			with or without the switch's own Username:/
			Password: dialog after the SSH login, with or
			without a "Building configuration..." banner, one
			or two "exit"s to end the session)
	aruba-cx-ssh	Aruba CX switches (AOS-CX; SSH; observed on
			PL.10.17). Also backs up HPE Aruba Networking
			wireless access points running AOS-8 - the CLI
			is close enough that the same model works
			(confirmed on an AP model 515, AOS-8 8.13.2.2).
	nexus-ssh	Cisco Nexus switches (NX-OS; SSH; observed on
			10.4(7)). Administrator account required - NX-OS
			has no "enable".
	mediant-sbc	AudioCodes Mediant SBC / gateway with the current
			Cisco-style CLI ("Welcome to AudioCodes CLI";
			observed on a Mediant 500Li). SSH by default,
			telnet or automatic fallback by option. Not the
			older menu-style CLI - that is "mediant".
	procurve-snmp	HP ProCurve ProVision switches, including the
			older 4000M/8000M chassis, backed up over
			SNMP+TFTP (no CLI needed). See PROCURVE SNMP
			BACKUPS below.

	tplink-web-sg105e
			TP-Link Easy Smart switches (TL-SG105E,
			TL-SG105PE and siblings, HTTP web UI - these
			switches have no CLI). See WEB UI BACKUPS below.
	procurve-web-1700
			HP ProCurve 1700 series (1700-8, 1700-24; HTTP
			web UI - web-managed only, no CLI). See WEB UI
			BACKUPS below.

	Note on hostnames with non-ASCII characters. aruba-cx-ssh and
	planet-ssh build their prompt regular expression in a byte-safe
	way and handle a device prompt containing spaces or non-ASCII
	characters (e.g. an umlaut in the hostname). The following
	models still build it with Perl's quotemeta(), which escapes
	each byte of a multi-byte UTF-8 character separately and would
	therefore fail to match such a prompt: procurve, procurve-ssh,
	hirschmann, comware-ssh and nec-univerge-ix. This has not been
	changed because it has not been observed on a real device;
	switching those models to the byte-safe quoting requires testing
	against real devices first. Until then, keep hostnames on those
	devices to ASCII.


TEMPLATE-DRIVEN MODEL (GENERIC)

	Besides the per-device Perl models listed above, fetchconfig has a
	"generic" model whose device interaction is described by a TEMPLATE
	file instead of Perl code. A template is a small state machine of
	expect/send steps with the transport, prompt, timeouts, interrupt
	screens and ignore-lines as data. For a clean-CLI device this is a
	short file rather than a ~200-line module, and a family of similar
	devices can share or clone one template.

	A device selects it in the device table with the model "generic" and
	a template name, for example:

		generic  sw1  10.0.0.1  model=cisco-ios,transport=ssh,user=...

	This loads templates/cisco-ios.tmpl. transport is ssh or telnet.
	Templates for Cisco IOS/IOS-XE (both transports) and HP ProCurve
	(menu/VT100) are provided as worked examples.

	Two companion tools support the workflow: tools/record-session.pl
	captures a real telnet/SSH session as a byte-accurate log to build a
	template from, and tools/generate_template.pl scaffolds a template
	draft from such a capture.

	The generic model and its tools are documented separately:

	  README.generic-model    the generic model and template grammar,
	                          quick reference
	  README.template_engine  the full engineering reference: architecture,
	                          grammar, execution semantics, writing and
	                          generating templates
	  README.record-session   the record-session.pl capture tool

	The template parser is production-ready; the execution engine has been
	verified on Cisco IOS-XE over telnet and SSH and on an HP ProCurve
	menu switch. Verify a template against the target device before relying
	on it.


TROUBLESHOOTING

	Note: some older SSH implementations (e.g. older HP/Aruba
	ProCurve firmware) only offer legacy key exchange algorithms
	that modern OpenSSH clients disable by default. fetchconfig.pl
	itself already re-enables what it needs for its own SSH
	connections (see the "KexAlgorithms"/"HostKeyAlgorithms"/
	"Ciphers" master_opts in ProCurveSSH.pm and ComwareSSH.pm), so
	this only matters if you want to connect to the same device
	manually with the system's own "ssh" command (e.g. while
	troubleshooting) - the same kind of algorithm may need to be
	re-enabled in the SSH client's own configuration:

	---[/etc/ssh_config]------------------------------
	Host *
	    KexAlgorithms +diffie-hellman-group1-sha1
	---[/etc/ssh_config]------------------------------


OPTIONS

	The cisco-ios, cisco-cat, cisco-asa and cisco-pix modules
	(CiscoIOS, CiscoCAT, CiscoASA and CiscoPIX) recognize the
	following options:

	pass		A mandatory option, specifies the login password.

	user		Specifies the login username. It can be omitted
                        for devices not requiring a login username.

	enable		Mandatory, specifies the enable password.

	timeout		How long, in seconds, to wait for responses from
                        the device. Defaults to 30 seconds if not set on
                        the device line or the model's "default:" line;
                        the default is applied by fetchconfig itself and
                        logged at debug level ("Using 30s fetch timeout
                        because the timeout was not set for this model").
                        Before 9.55 an unset timeout fell through to the
                        underlying library's own default (10 s for telnet/
                        SSH via Net::Telnet, 180 s for the web-UI models),
                        so set it explicitly for anything but a lab test.

	fetch_timeout	Optional. If given, will override the timeout
                        value used to fetch all the configuration lines.
                        It is useful for devices which spend several
                        seconds building the configuration.

	repository	Mandatory, specifies the base directory for
                        saving the configuration files.

	keep		Mandatory, the maximum number of config files
                        to retain for the device. When this limit is
                        reached, the older files are discarded.

	changes_only 	Optional. Boolean. If specified as
			changes_only=1, only new configurations are
			saved. Otherwise, configurations are saved
			whenever the script runs; if not defined as
			changes_only=1, the script might possibly
			retain multiple identical configuration files.

	on_fetch_run	Optional. Path to an external program to be
			called whenever the configuration is fetched.
			The following environmental variables are
			passed to the program:
			FETCHCONFIG_DEV_ID = device unique id
			FETCHCONFIG_DEV_HOST = device hostname (or IP addr)
			FETCHCONFIG_PREV = pathname to previous config,
                                           undefined if none exists
			FETCHCONFIG_CURR = pathname to current config
			Example:
			on_fetch_run=/usr/local/bin/mail_diff_to_admin.sh
			Please notice that, if changes_only=1, the
			backup file referenced to FETCHCONFIG_CURR
			will be erased right after the external program
			is finished. This option is available for all
			modules.

	on_fetch_cat	Optional. Boolean. If specified as
			on_fetch_cat=1, the currently fetched
			configuration is issued to standard
			output. This option is available for all
			modules.

	timezone	Optional. If defined as timezone=hide, it will
			supress timezone information from config filename.

	filename_append_suffix
			Optional. If defined, it is appended to the
			generated backup filename, which then reads
			<dev_id>.run.YYYYMMDD.HHMMSS<tz><suffix>. This
			option is available for all modules.
			Example: filename_append_suffix=.txt

			WARNING - fetchconfig 9.x and later only accept a
			suffix that starts with a "." (a file extension,
			e.g. ".txt" or ".cfg"). A suffix without the
			leading dot, or containing "/" or whitespace,
			makes that device's backup fail with an error and
			nothing is written. If you upgraded from an older
			version with a dotless suffix in your device
			table, fix it before the first 9.x run.

			Do NOT add, remove or change the suffix once
			backups of a device exist. Backup files are found
			by their <dev_id>.run. prefix, so old and new
			names would coexist in the same directory: keep=N
			expires them together and the "latest" ordering
			can be affected. Decide on the suffix before the
			first backup. Use "fetchconfig.pl -s dev_id" (one
			device) or "-S" (all devices) to check whether a
			device already has a mix of suffixed and unsuffixed
			(or differently suffixed) backups.

	to		Optional. Overrides, for this device only, who
			receives the backup summary e-mail configured
			by the "email:" line. See EMAIL NOTIFICATIONS
			above. This option is available for all
			modules.

	comment		Optional. Free-text note about the device,
			purely for the benefit of whoever is reading
			the device table - it is not used anywhere
			else (not logged, not e-mailed, not saved into
			the backup file). This option is available for
			all modules.
			Example: comment=lab switch

	Quoting option values: any option value (comment= or
	otherwise) may be wrapped in double quotes, e.g.
	comment="Building 3, Floor 2". This is only needed if the
	value itself contains a literal comma, since a comma
	otherwise separates one option from the next; quoting is
	never required just because a value contains spaces. Inside
	a quoted value, \" and \\ represent a literal quote and a
	literal backslash. Quoting is optional, not enforced - an
	unquoted value with a comma in it is still just split at
	that comma, exactly as before.

	Options specific to CiscoIOS:

	show_cmd	Optional. If specified, its value will replace
			the usual "show run" command. This is necessary
                        for some older devices. It may be useful to
			send alternative commands to capture the
			configuration. To keep compatibility with
			previous versions of fetchconfig, if specified
			as show_cmd=wrterm, "write term" will be used
			in place of the usual "show run" command.

	The cisco-ios-ssh and cisco-asa-ssh modules recognize the
	following options:
	(See above cisco-ios/cisco-cat section for descriptions. These
	are the SSH counterparts of cisco-ios and cisco-asa; the login
	is done over SSH with user/pass, and the shell is then driven
	the same way as the telnet models.)

	user		Mandatory. SSH login name.
	pass		Mandatory. SSH login password.
	enable		Optional. The enable password, sent if the
			account lands on the ">" prompt.
	repository	Mandatory.
	keep		Mandatory.
	timeout		Optional, default 30 seconds (see the cisco-ios/
                        cisco-cat section).
	fetch_timeout	Optional.
	changes_only	Optional.
	on_fetch_run	Optional.
	on_fetch_cat	Optional.
	timezone	Optional.
	filename_append_suffix Optional.
	show_cmd	Optional. If specified, its value will replace
			the usual "show run" command.
	debug		Optional. Boolean. If specified as debug=on
			(default off), records to <repository>/<dev_id>.debug
			(mode 0600) first the ssh client's account of the
			SSH authentication and then the raw device session
			(since 9.59; before, these two models wrote no debug
			file).

