WEB UI BACKUPS (TP-LINK EASY SMART, PROCURVE 1700)

	Some switches have no CLI at all - TP-Link Easy Smart switches
	(TL-SG105E, TL-SG105PE and siblings) and the HP ProCurve 1700
	series. The tplink-web-sg105e and procurve-web-1700 models
	retrieve the config the same way a browser does on the
	switch's config backup page: they log in over plain HTTP (the
	switches offer no HTTPS), download the config file, and log
	out again. These models share their HTTP plumbing
	(AbstractHTTP.pm); the login, download and logout requests
	are per device, taken from packet captures of real browser
	sessions.

	TP-Link: both session mechanisms seen on real devices are
	handled - the TL-SG105E binds the login session to the
	client's IP address, the TL-SG105PE v2 uses a session cookie.
	Either way the switch only allows a limited number of
	concurrent logins; if it reports the login table as full, the
	backup of that device is reported as failed rather than
	forcing another user out.

	ProCurve 1700: the switch has only a manager password (the
	user option is ignored) and binds the session to the client's
	IP address. The config file it hands out ("switch.cfg")
	contains the SNMP communities and, apparently, the manager
	password in clear - protect the repository accordingly.

	The downloaded config is an opaque binary file, not text. It
	is stored uuencoded ("begin 644 <name>.cfg" ... "end") so that
	it fits the text-based repository, the changes_only comparison
	and the -g retrieval like any other backup. To turn a backup
	back into the file the switch's restore page expects:

		uudecode -o config.cfg <backup-file>
	or
		fetchconfig.pl -devices=... -g <dev_id> | uudecode -o config.cfg

	A line-by-line diff between two backups (-m) is possible but
	not meaningful for these models - it only shows that, not
	what, changed.


	The generic module (GenericTemplate) recognizes the following
	options. It is the template-driven model (see TEMPLATE-DRIVEN
	MODEL above and README.generic-model); most of the timing and
	prompt options below may also be set in the template file, and
	a device-table option overrides the template value of the same
	name.

	model		Mandatory. The template to load: templates/<model>.tmpl
			(e.g. model=cisco-ios). The only option with no
			template-file equivalent.
	transport	Which transport to use, "ssh" or "telnet". If omitted,
			the template's first-listed transport is used.
	user		Login username. Mandatory for SSH; for telnet it is
			sent at the device's "Username:" prompt if the
			template's login sequence uses it.
	pass		Login password. Mandatory for SSH; for telnet as above.
	enable		Enable password, if the template enters enable mode.
	repository	Mandatory. Base directory for saving the config files.
	keep		Optional. How many backups to keep per device.
	changes_only	Optional. Only save when the config changed since the
			last run (honouring the template's "ignore" lines).
	timeout		Optional, default 30 seconds (see the cisco-ios/
			cisco-cat section). May be set in the template.
	fetch_timeout	Optional. Timeout for reading the show output. May be
			set in the template.
	banner_timeout	Optional, default 30. Seconds to wait for the first
			prompt after connecting. May be set in the template.
	fetch_delay	Optional, default 0. Seconds to pause after connecting
			before interacting. May be set in the template.
	prompt_settle	Optional. Prompt settle time in milliseconds. May be
			set in the template.
	max_visits	Optional, default 8. Global cap on how many times a
			template state may be re-entered (bounds self-loops).
			May be set in the template.
	strip_ansi	Optional, "yes" or "no" (default no). Strip VT100/ANSI
			escape sequences from device output. May be set in
			the template.
	prompt_head	Optional. Regex matched before the learned hostname
			when building the prompt pattern. May be set in the
			template.
	prompt_tail	Optional, default "#". Regex matched after the learned
			hostname. May be set in the template.
	ssh_extra_opts	Optional. Extra options passed to the ssh client
			(e.g. legacy key-exchange/cipher settings for old
			switches). May be set in the template.
	template_dir	Optional. Directory to load the template from. An
			absolute path, or relative to the current working
			directory. Defaults to the templates/ directory that
			ships beside fetchconfig.pl (located regardless of the
			working directory), so this is only needed to load
			templates from elsewhere.
	debug		Optional. debug=on writes a 0600 session log to
			<repository>/<dev_id>.debug (passwords masked).
	on_fetch_run	Optional (see the cisco-ios/cisco-cat section).
	on_fetch_cat	Optional (see the cisco-ios/cisco-cat section).
	timezone	Optional (see the cisco-ios/cisco-cat section).
	filename_append_suffix	Optional (see the cisco-ios/cisco-cat
			section).
	comment		Optional (see the cisco-ios/cisco-cat section).

	The following are set only in the template file, not on the
	device line: capture_from (regex marking where the saved config
	starts) and skip_first (drop the first N captured lines). See
	README.generic-model for the full template grammar.


PROCURVE SNMP BACKUPS

	The older HP ProCurve ProVision chassis (4000M, 8000M) can only
	be managed remotely through a VT100 menu, which is not usable
	for scripted backup. They will, however, export a text copy of
	their configuration over TFTP when told to over SNMP, and that
	is what procurve-snmp does. It is a transport unlike the other
	models: no shell and no web UI, but SNMP plus TFTP, and hence no
	user/pass - the credential is the SNMP read-write community.

	The sequence is: an SNMP set arms the switch's TFTP export, the
	config is fetched from the switch by TFTP, and a second SNMP set
	disables the export again. That disable always runs, even when
	the transfer failed, so the switch is never left with its TFTP
	server open.

	The default remote_file "browse" produces a human-readable
	report (the "System Information / Port Settings / ..." listing).
	That is fine for backup and change tracking - which is what
	fetchconfig does - but it is a display export, not a file that
	can be TFTP'd back to restore the switch. Later ProVision models
	also accept remote_file=running-config, which is a replayable
	config; set the option accordingly on those.

	Note on firewalls: the switch pushes the file to the backup host
	over TFTP (UDP/69 inbound, then a data port). A host firewall
	on the backup machine can block this; it then shows up as a TFTP
	get failure or timeout, not a hang.

PARALLEL FETCHING

	Fetching is sequential unless asked otherwise: one device at a
	time, in device-table order, with every log line shown as it
	happens. That is the right default for a handful of devices and
	for troubleshooting, but with thousands of devices the run time
	is dominated by the network sessions (roughly 5-15 seconds each,
	and a full timeout for every unreachable device), and a
	sequential run no longer fits in a night.

		./fetchconfig.pl -devices=device_table -P 20

	"-P N" fetches with N concurrent worker processes. The parent
	process loads the device table, then hands out one device at a
	time to whichever worker asks next, so a slow device never holds
	up the others. Every worker is a copy of the fully loaded
	parent, so the models run unchanged. With N=10-20 a run of
	several hours typically completes in well under one; the gain is
	bounded by what the devices and the network tolerate, not by the
	backup host.

	Output is atomic per device. While a worker fetches, everything
	it writes to stdout and stderr is buffered; when the device is
	finished the parent copies that buffer to the real stdout/stderr
	in one uninterrupted block. The output of one device is therefore
	never mixed with the output of another - each device's block
	looks exactly as it does in a sequential run (the "-----[dev]"
	banner, its log lines, any on_fetch_cat output). Two consequences
	of buffering: blocks appear in COMPLETION order, not device-table
	order; and a device's lines appear when it finishes, not live. At
	"-P 1" (the default) nothing is buffered and behaviour is
	unchanged from earlier versions.

	Everything else is unaffected by concurrency: each device writes
	only its own directory, .status file and .debug file; the summary
	e-mail, the per-device to= bundling and the .status files are all
	produced by the parent from the workers' results, exactly as in a
	sequential run; keep=N expiry and changes_only comparison run per
	device as before. A worker that dies (perl error, killed, out of
	memory) is detected by the parent; the device it was fetching is
	recorded as failed (in the mail and its .status file) and the
	remaining devices are still fetched by the other workers.

	Sizing N. Start at 10 and raise it while watching for devices
	that refuse or delay logins: the limits are the switches'
	concurrent-session caps, the TACACS+/RADIUS server's rate, and
	the links to remote sites - not the backup host. 10-25 is the
	typical range. "-P" applies to fetching only; it cannot be
	combined with the lookup/maintenance modes (-g, -l, -z, -Z, -o,
	-e, -s, -S), which stay single-process.

SECURITY NOTES

	Debug files contain credentials. With debug=on, the per-device
	debug file <repository>/<dev_id>.debug records the session
	verbatim - for the telnet/SSH models that includes the login and
	enable passwords exactly as typed, and for procurve-snmp the SNMP
	read-write community. For the SSH models the file begins with the
	ssh client's debug trace (LogLevel=DEBUG, the same output as
	"ssh -v": key exchange, host key, authentication methods tried,
	"Authentication succeeded"); ssh never writes the password itself
	into that trace. Starting with 9.54 these files are created
	with mode 0600 (owner read/write only) regardless of the process
	umask. Still: only enable debug=on while troubleshooting, and
	delete the .debug file afterwards. Backups and the .status file
	are created with the process umask (typically 0644); the backups
	do contain configuration secrets (SNMP communities, password
	hashes), so restrict the repository directory itself if the host
	is shared.

	SSH host keys are not verified. The SSH models connect with
	StrictHostKeyChecking=no and UserKnownHostsFile=/dev/null, i.e.
	any host key is accepted and none is remembered. This is
	deliberate: fetchconfig runs as an unattended job, and a switch's
	host key changes on every hardware swap, which would otherwise
	stop the backup until someone edited known_hosts. The
	consequence is that an attacker on the network path could
	impersonate a device and capture the login password. Run
	fetchconfig on a trusted management network.

	Device IDs are validated. A dev_id may contain letters, digits,
	underscore, dot and dash (so hostnames like sw01.example.com are
	fine) but may not consist only of dots, since "." or ".." would
	resolve to the repository itself or its parent.

	SMTP. AUTH LOGIN sends the password base64-encoded, not
	encrypted; see the "tls" option under EMAIL NOTIFICATIONS. Header
	fields (From, To, Subject) have line breaks stripped before being
	sent, so a device-table value cannot inject extra mail headers.

BACKUP FILE NAMING

	Backups are named <dev_id>.run.YYYYMMDD.HHMMSS[<tz>][<suffix>],
	where <suffix> is the optional filename_append_suffix (must start
	with a "."; see the OPTIONS section), i.e. with one-second
	resolution.

	<tz> is whatever strftime produced on the backup host and so
	differs by platform and changes with daylight saving time: a
	numeric offset ("+0200", "-0500") on Linux and modern Windows;
	a bare name ("CEST" in summer, "CET" in winter; "NFT"/"DFT" on
	older AIX) on AIX; "-CEST" on Solaris; a multi-word name such as
	"W. Europe Standard Time" (or a localized one) on older Windows;
	or nothing with timezone=hide. Since 9.55 every operation that
	orders backups ("latest" for changes_only, keep=N expiry, -g/-n/
	-m, -l, -z/-Z, -s/-S) orders them by the PARSED timestamp
	YYYYMMDD.HHMMSS rather than by a plain string sort of the whole
	name, so neither the tz token nor a suffix can disturb the order.
	Two backups of the same device completed within the same second
	would get the same name; the second would overwrite the first,
	and the changes_only comparison would then compare the file with
	itself. This is an accepted limitation: a real fetch (login,
	pager, full configuration, logout) essentially never completes
	in under a second, and scheduled runs are minutes apart. It
	only shows up in artificial back-to-back test runs.

STATUS FILE

	After every backup attempt, fetchconfig writes a small status
	file next to the backups, at:

		<repository>/<dev_id>.status

	It is written for every device and every model, and does not
	depend on the "email:" line - it is a plain repository artifact,
	meant for external tools (for example a web front-end that reads
	backup_duration_sec to size a progress spinner). It is
	overwritten on every run and written atomically, so a reader
	never sees a half-written file.

	The format is INI-style: a "[<dev_id>]" section header, then
	key=value lines:

		[swi-lab-01]
		backup_start_date=2026-09-11
		backup_start_time=22:14:03
		backup_end_date=2026-09-11
		backup_end_time=22:14:09
		backup_duration_sec=6
		backup_state=success
		backup_changed=changed

	Fields:

	backup_start_date / backup_start_time
			When the fetch started (local time). Derived as
			end minus backup_duration_sec.
	backup_end_date / backup_end_time
			When the fetch finished (local time).
	backup_duration_sec
			Whole seconds the fetch took - the same value
			logged for the run and shown in the summary
			e-mail.
	backup_state	success, failed, or skipped.
	backup_changed	unchanged or changed for a changes_only=1
			device; forced for a changes_only=0 device (the
			backup is saved regardless of any comparison);
			unknown when the state is failed or skipped.

				 -- END --

